<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ruthwell Cross</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ruthwell Cross is an eighth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> stone cross now inside the church of Ruthwell in Dumfriesshire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Its carved panels show scenes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> inscriptions, and its sides bear runic verses related to the Old English poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-dream-of-the-rood">The Dream of the Rood</a>. Broken up by order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a> in the 1640s, it was reassembled in the nineteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ruthwell-cross.jpg" alt="Ruthwell Cross" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lairich Rig · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Ruthwell_Cross_-_geograph.org.uk_-_939546.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The cross stands 18 feet (5.5 m) high and was made when Ruthwell lay in the Anglo-Saxon Kingdom of Northumbria. It belongs to the Insular art tradition, with close links to the Irish high crosses, and carries the largest figurative reliefs on any surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> cross. Nikolaus Pevsner set the crosses of Bewcastle and Ruthwell above any work of their date in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Most scholars hold that its images and texts form one learned programme, though they propose different schemes for it.</p><p>The largest panel, on the north side, shows Christ either treading on the beasts or recognised as judge by the beasts in the desert, as its inscription suggests; the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bewcastle-cross">Bewcastle Cross</a> has a very similar relief. Below are Saints Paul and Antony breaking bread in the desert and a Flight into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The south side shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-magdalene">Mary Magdalene</a> drying the feet of Christ, the Healing of the man born blind and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annunciation">Annunciation</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crucifixion">Crucifixion</a> is thought on stylistic grounds to be a later addition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The cross survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, but in 1640 the General Assembly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a> ordered idolatrous monuments destroyed, and in 1642 it was taken down and broken; one piece appears to have served as a bench. By 1823 Henry Duncan had collected the pieces, commissioned a new crossbeam and set it up in the manse garden; the side arms and centre roundel are speculative replacements, and the two sections may have been restored the wrong way round. In 1887 it was moved into an apse built for it inside Ruthwell church.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Reginald Bainbrigg of Appleby recorded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/runes">runes</a> around 1600 for William Camden's Britannia. Around 1832 Thorleif Repp recognised them as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> rather than Scandinavian, and John Mitchell Kemble proposed the reading linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-dream-of-the-rood">The Dream of the Rood</a> in 1842, filling gaps from the Vercelli Book; his reading is disputed. Many scholars think the runes were added later, perhaps as late as the 10th century. Fred Orton and Patrick W. Conner have argued that the monument may first have been a column rather than a cross. The Visionary Cross project made 3D scans of it in 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ruthwell Cross” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The stone crosses of <b>Ruthwell</b> and Bewcastle carry some of the most ambitious sculpture of the period, and the Ruthwell Cross bears runic verses related to the poem The Dream of the Rood.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
